  "f2p_absence": {
    "audited_paths": [],
    "reason": "The defect is that one painted layer is indistinguishable from the surface behind it under one theme, because both resolve to the same colour. Every value involved is a stylesheet variable the document never reads, so the collision is observable only once painted."
  },
  "fix_commit": "1f18828225e5e16c569dcb810eb4c6137cd33d9b",
  "issue": {
    "active_lock_reason": "resolved",
    "assignee": null,
    "assignees": [],
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body_normalized": "<!-- Welcome! Thank you for contributing. These HTML comments will not render in the issue.\n\nBefore creating a new issue:\n* Search for relevant issues\n* Follow the issue reporting guidelines:\nhttps://jupyterlab.readthedocs.io/en/latest/getting_started/issue.html\n-->\n\n## Description\n\n<!--Describe the bug clearly and concisely. Include screenshots if possible-->\nUnable to select text, commands, console outputs etc. within terminal window inside Jupyter Lab locally. The same selections work as expected in Notebook window. In order to be able to use console outputs or edit commands to be executed, the ability to select pieces of text is essential. Being unable to do so can cause unnecessary bottlenecks and degrade user experience.\n\n## Reproduce\n\n<!--Describe step-by-step instructions to reproduce the behavior-->\n\n1. Open Jupyter Lab locally after installing JupyterLab for your machine\n2. Once open in browser, click on the terminal icon to open a new terminal session\n3. Run commands to display meaningful console outputs (for example, ls)\n4. Attempt to select the console output or commands by left clicking mouse/trackpad and dragging\n5. The area dragged over is not selected\n\n<!--Describe how you diagnosed the issue. See the guidelines at\n https://jupyterlab.readthedocs.io/en/latest/getting_started/issue.html -->\n\n## Expected behavior\n\n<!--Describe what you expected to happen-->\nFollowing the steps outlined in the section on how to reproduce, we should ideally be able to select the items displayed in terminal window to be able to perform Copy/Paste.\n\n## Context\n\n<!--Complete the following for context, and add any other relevant context-->\n\n- Operating System and version: <!-- e.g. Linux Ubuntu 21.04 --> MacOS Sequoia 15.3.2\n- Browser and version: <!-- e.g. Chrome 92 --> Google Chrome, version 134.0.6998.165\n- JupyterLab version: <!-- e.g. 3.1.7 --> JupyterLab 4.4.0rc0\n\n<!--The more content you provide, the more we can help!-->",
    "body_raw": "<!-- Welcome! Thank you for contributing. These HTML comments will not render in the issue.\n\nBefore creating a new issue:\n* Search for relevant issues\n* Follow the issue reporting guidelines:\nhttps://jupyterlab.readthedocs.io/en/latest/getting_started/issue.html\n-->\n\n## Description\n\n<!--Describe the bug clearly and concisely. Include screenshots if possible-->\nUnable to select text, commands, console outputs etc. within terminal window inside Jupyter Lab locally. The same selections work as expected in Notebook window. In order to be able to use console outputs or edit commands to be executed, the ability to select pieces of text is essential. Being unable to do so can cause unnecessary bottlenecks and degrade user experience.\n\n## Reproduce\n\n<!--Describe step-by-step instructions to reproduce the behavior-->\n\n1. Open Jupyter Lab locally after installing JupyterLab for your machine\n2. Once open in browser, click on the terminal icon to open a new terminal session\n3. Run commands to display meaningful console outputs (for example, ls)\n4. Attempt to select the console output or commands by left clicking mouse/trackpad and dragging\n5. The area dragged over is not selected \n\n<!--Describe how you diagnosed the issue. See the guidelines at\n https://jupyterlab.readthedocs.io/en/latest/getting_started/issue.html -->\n\n## Expected behavior\n\n<!--Describe what you expected to happen-->\nFollowing the steps outlined in the section on how to reproduce, we should ideally be able to select the items displayed in terminal window to be able to perform Copy/Paste.\n\n## Context\n\n<!--Complete the following for context, and add any other relevant context-->\n\n- Operating System and version: <!-- e.g. Linux Ubuntu 21.04 --> MacOS Sequoia 15.3.2\n- Browser and version: <!-- e.g. Chrome 92 --> Google Chrome, version 134.0.6998.165\n- JupyterLab version: <!-- e.g. 3.1.7 --> JupyterLab 4.4.0rc0\n\n<!--The more content you provide, the more we can help!-->\n",
